.TH SYSREPORT-SEARCH 1 "2026-10-31" "srs-sysinfo 2.5.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sysreport-search \- search a native system report, render report items and look up PCI/USB IDs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sysreport-search
[\fB\-h\fR] [\fB\-\-version\fR] {\fBid\fR,\fBfind\fR,\fBdevices\fR,\fB\-save\fR,\fB\-\-list\fR,\fBguide\fR,\fBburn\fR,\fBbench\fR,\fBipi\fR,\fBlatency\fR,\fBpi\fR,\fBcrypto\fR,\fBprime\fR,\fBcachemem\fR,\fBstorage\fR,\fBos\-latency\fR,\fBcompute\fR,\fBwork\fR,\fBnet\fR,\fBgpu\fR,\fBgpu\-burn\fR,\fBgpu\-compare\fR,\fBcuda\-toolkit\fR,\fBgpu\-top\fR,\fBbig\-top\fR,\fBir\fR,\fBupdate\-lists\fR} ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
SIV-style search for the native system report written by
.BR srs-sysreport (1),
device-ID lookups against the hwdata databases, the report items of
.BR srs-gui (1)
as text, and the CPU benchmarks and stress test.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
\fBid\fR \fIVVVV\fR[:\fIDDDD\fR[:\fISSSS\fR:\fIssss\fR]][:\fIRR\fR] [\fB\-\-bus\fR pci|usb] [\fB\-\-mode\fR tree|scan|info]
hierarchical device-ID lookup, most specific key first.
.TP
\fBfind\fR \fIPATTERN\fR [\fB\-\-report\fR \fIDIR\fR] [\fB\-i\fR] [\fB\-F\fR] [\fB\-\-max\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-color\fR auto|always|never]
regular-expression search across a report's sections with highlighted matches.
.TP
\fBdevices\fR [\fB\-\-bus\fR pci|usb|all]
resolve every local PCI/USB device.
.TP
\fB\-save\fR[=[\fIitem\fR][\fIitem\fR:\fIarg\fR]...][=\fIFILE\fR|STDOUT] [\fB\-\-redact\fR]
render report items as text (no items = the default set, [all] = every item).
\fB\-\-redact\fR masks serial numbers, UUIDs, MAC addresses (vendor half kept),
boot/machine IDs and the host name, for reports you share.
Quote the argument in zsh or fish ([..] is a glob there).
.TP
\fB\-\-list\fR
list every item: id, category, bucket, group, title.
.TP
\fBguide\fR [\fIITEM\fR] [\fB\-\-print\fR]
open the user guide at \fIITEM\fR's section (PDF viewer, else the HTML guide);
\fB\-\-print\fR only shows the section and the command that would run.
.TP
\fBburn\fR [\fB\-\-threads\fR \fIN\fR | \fB\-\-cpus\fR \fILIST\fR] [\fB\-\-seconds\fR \fIS\fR] [\fB\-\-mode\fR same|swap] [\fB\-\-limit\fR \fIC\fR] [\fB\-\-accept\-high\-limit\fR] [\fB\-\-cool\fR \fIS\fR] [\fB\-\-cycles\fR \fIN\fR]
CPU burn-in with a temperature limit (default 10 s, limit 90 C; \fB\-\-limit\fR 50..100, or 101..110 together with \fB\-\-accept\-high\-limit\fR: above 100 C many CPUs and GPUs are already at or near their own maximum, and using it means you accept that risk; any other value exits 2 without starting).
The summary of each run is saved to \fI~/.cache/srs/burnin\-YYYYMMDD\-HHMMSS.txt\fR.
.TP
\fBbench\fR {ac,as,dd,dw,ec,ep,lc,lp,pc,pp} [\fB\-\-seconds\fR \fIS\fR] [\fB\-\-yes\fR]
Dhrystone / Whetstone in a SIV benchmark mode (0.1..60 s per measurement); asks first unless \fB\-\-yes\fR.
.TP
\fBipi\fR [\fB\-\-cpus\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-rounds\fR \fIR\fR] [\fB\-\-yes\fR]
core-to-core (cache-line ping-pong) latency matrix (two CPUs busy at a time); asks first unless \fB\-\-yes\fR. With one CPU there is no pair: it exits with 2 and keeps the last result.
.TP
\fBlatency\fR [\fB\-\-max\fR \fIMB\fR] [\fB\-\-yes\fR]
memory latency by block size, up to \fIMB\fR (1..4096, default 256; one CPU); asks first unless \fB\-\-yes\fR.
.TP
\fBpi\fR [\fB\-\-digits\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-threads\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-method\fR \fBchudnovsky\fR|\fBagm\fR] [\fB\-\-save\-digits\fR \fIFILE\fR] [\fB\-\-yes\fR]
SRS Pi: pi to \fIN\fR decimal places (128k..32m, or SuperPi sizes 16ki..32mi; default 1m, 1 thread); every digit is checked. Asks first, with the time, the threads and the memory it needs (up to about 1.6 GB for 32mi), unless \fB\-\-yes\fR.
.TP
\fBcrypto\fR [\fB\-\-threads\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-seconds\fR \fIS\fR] [\fB\-\-all\-threads\-only\fR] [\fB\-\-yes\fR]
AES\-128 and SHA\-256 speed in MB/s, with AES\-NI / SHA\-NI and in plain C; asks first unless \fB\-\-yes\fR. \fB\-\-all\-threads\-only\fR (also on \fBcompute\fR and \fBwork\fR) skips the 1\-thread pass like the GUI's All Cores: the 1\-thread column says "skipped" and the run history has "\-" there and \fB1\-THREAD\-SKIPPED\fR.
.TP
\fBprime\fR [\fB\-\-limit\fR \fB1e8\fR|\fB1e9\fR|\fB1e10\fR|\fB1e11\fR|\fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-threads\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-yes\fR]
count the primes up to the limit (any number from 100 to 1e11, e.g. 5e9 or 1g; default 1e9, all threads), checked against the known count; asks first unless \fB\-\-yes\fR.
.TP
\fBcachemem\fR [\fB\-\-threads\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-seconds\fR \fIS\fR] [\fB\-\-isa\fR \fBavx512\fR|\fBavx2\fR|\fBsse2\fR] [\fB\-\-mem\-mib\fR \fIM\fR] [\fB\-\-only\fR \fIlevels\fR] [\fB\-\-tests\fR \fItests\fR] [\fB\-\-mib\fR] [\fB\-\-mem\-write\fR \fImode\fR] [\fB\-\-yes\fR]
Cache & Memory Benchmark: Read / Write / Copy and Latency of the RAM and L1\-L3 (all threads, about 20 s; asks first unless \fB\-\-yes\fR).
.TP
\fBstorage\fR \fB\-\-dir\fR \fIFOLDER\fR [\fB\-\-size\fR \fIMB\fR] [\fB\-\-seconds\fR \fIS\fR] [\fB\-\-profile\fR \fBstandard\fR|\fBlight\fR] [\fB\-\-allow\-usb\fR] [\fB\-\-yes\fR]
read / write speed of the drive holding \fIFOLDER\fR, on a temporary test file that is unlinked at once; existing files are never touched; asks first unless \fB\-\-yes\fR.
.TP
\fBos\-latency\fR [\fB\-\-samples\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-tick\-seconds\fR \fIS\fR] [\fB\-\-only\fR \fIgroups\fR] [\fB\-\-yes\fR]
how quickly the OS answers: system calls, context switches, thread / process start, wake\-ups, timer lateness (with the CPU scheduler in use), page faults and small\-file create / stat / unlink, as median / 95% / 99% / worst (one or two CPUs, about 6 s; asks first unless \fB\-\-yes\fR). \fIgroups\fR: syscall, switch, spawn, wake, timer, memory, files.
.TP
\fBcompute\fR [\fB\-\-threads\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-seconds\fR \fIS\fR] [\fB\-\-tests\fR \fItests\fR] [\fB\-\-code\fR \fBauto\fR|\fBc\fR|\fBsse2\fR|\fBavx2\fR|\fBavx512\fR] [\fB\-\-single\fR | \fB\-\-all\-threads\-only\fR] [\fB\-\-yes\fR]
CPU Compute: matrix multiply, peak multiply\-add, FFT, N\-body, Mandelbrot and a ray tracer in GFLOPS / Mpixels/s on 1 and N threads, every answer checked (WRONG instead of a score). All threads are 100% busy (AVX\-512 where available; an unstable overclock or undervolt may crash the PC); \fB\-\-single\fR keeps it light; asks first unless \fB\-\-yes\fR.
.TP
\fBwork\fR [\fB\-\-threads\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-seconds\fR \fIS\fR] [\fB\-\-only\fR \fIgroups\fR] [\fB\-\-libs\fR \fIlibs\fR] [\fB\-\-single\fR | \fB\-\-all\-threads\-only\fR] [\fB\-\-part\-kib\fR \fIK\fR] [\fB\-\-yes\fR]
Everyday work: compression with the system's zlib, zstd, lz4 and xz (loaded at run time; a missing one is skipped), sorting, checksums, text processing and a bytecode interpreter on 1 and N threads, every result checked; generated data only; asks first unless \fB\-\-yes\fR. \fIgroups\fR: compress, sort, hash, text, interp.
.TP
\fBnet\fR [\fB\-\-local\fR] [\fB\-\-internet\fR] [\fB\-\-seconds\fR \fIS\fR] [\fB\-\-streams\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-samples\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-max\-mb\fR \fIMB\fR] [\fB\-4\fR|\fB\-6\fR] [\fB\-\-server\fR \fIURL\fR] [\fB\-\-hosts\fR \fIHOST\fR[:\fIPORT\fR],...] [\fB\-\-yes\fR] [\fB\-\-yes\-internet\fR]
Network Benchmark. Without options: loopback TCP / UDP throughput, loss and round trip time (nothing leaves the computer; asks first unless \fB\-\-yes\fR). \fB\-\-internet\fR adds DNS, connect, HTTPS, download and upload tests against https://speed.cloudflare.com; it prints what it contacts and the data use (up to about 4 x \fB\-\-max\-mb\fR, default 1000 MB) and asks its own question, which only \fB\-\-yes\-internet\fR skips. libcurl is optional (loaded at run time). No addresses or host names are shown or saved.
.TP
\fBgpu\fR [\fB\-\-device\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-seconds\fR \fIS\fR] [\fB\-\-only\fR \fItests\fR] [\fB\-\-mem\-mib\fR \fIM\fR] [\fB\-\-list\fR] [\fB\-\-yes\fR]
GPU Benchmark through Vulkan: FP32, FP16, FP64 and INT32 multiply\-add (GFLOPS / GIOPS), video memory read, write and copy, upload and download (GB/s) and dispatch latency (µs), every result checked. The Vulkan loader is opened at run time (optional). \fB\-\-list\fR lists the Vulkan devices and runs nothing. Asks first unless \fB\-\-yes\fR; exit code 2 when it did not start. \fItests\fR: fp32, fp16, fp64, int32, read, write, copy, upload, download, latency.
.TP
\fBgpu\-burn\fR [\fB\-\-seconds\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-backend\fR \fBvulkan\fR|\fBhip\fR|\fBcuda\fR|\fBopencl\fR|\fBauto\fR] [\fB\-\-device\fR \fIN\fR|\fBall\fR] [\fB\-\-fp32\fR|\fB\-\-fp64\fR] [\fB\-\-limit\fR \fIC\fR] [\fB\-\-accept\-high\-limit\fR] [\fB\-\-list\fR] [\fB\-\-yes\fR]
GPU Burnin: repeated matrix multiplications at full GPU load through Vulkan, ROCm/HIP, CUDA or OpenCL (all loaded at run time), every result compared with the first; 0 errors = PASS (exit 0), errors or an early end exit 1. Live temperature, power and clock on a terminal. Since 2.4 a register\-blocked matrix multiply on every back end loads the GPU much harder (the result's Kernel line says which ran; \fBSRS_GPUBURN_SIMPLE=1\fR forces the older one). Ends by itself at the temperature limit (\fB\-\-limit\fR 50..100, default 100 C; 101..110 only with \fB\-\-accept\-high\-limit\fR), on a driver error, when the GPU stops answering or a piece of work takes more than 2 s; Ctrl+C stops it. Never changes clocks, voltages or fans, and never burns a software renderer (llvmpipe) as a GPU. Default 60 s. Asks first unless \fB\-\-yes\fR; before that only Vulkan's device list is read. \fB\-\-list\fR lists each back end's devices (starts the runtimes, runs no work).
.TP
\fBgpu\-compare\fR [\fB\-\-backends\fR \fIvulkan,hip,cuda,opencl\fR] [\fB\-\-seconds\fR \fIS\fR] [\fB\-\-device\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-yes\fR]
GPU Compute comparison: the same tests (matrix multiply FP32 / FP16 / FP64 with SRS's own kernel and the vendor library, multiply\-add, copy, upload, download, launch round trip) through every installed back end on the same GPU, side by side; fastest marked, percent of Vulkan; "CUDA (ZLUDA)" and one column per OpenCL driver. CUDA uses SRS's built-in kernels (PTX), so the NVIDIA runtime compiler (NVRTC) is not needed; an OpenCL driver without FP64 shows its FP64 rows as "not available". Asks first unless \fB\-\-yes\fR.
.TP
\fBcuda\-toolkit\fR [\fB\-\-check\fR|\fB\-\-install\fR|\fB\-\-ask\-again\fR|\fB\-\-never\-ask\fR]
The optional NVIDIA CUDA toolkit (NVIDIA's developer tools; SRS can use its runtime compiler, NVRTC, as a fallback): what it is for, whether NVRTC and a CUDA library (NVIDIA's or ZLUDA) are installed, and the install command for this distribution (Arch: pacman \-S \-\-needed cuda; Debian/Ubuntu: apt\-get install libnvrtc12 or libnvrtc11.2; Fedora, openSUSE: instructions). \fB\-\-install\fR checks first (package database lock, network, sudo/pkexec), needs a terminal and runs the command with sudo only after a typed \fByes\fR (twice with ZLUDA, where installing it is not recommended). \fB\-\-never\-ask\fR / \fB\-\-ask\-again\fR: whether the GUI asks about it (and gpu\-burn / gpu\-compare print a note). Nothing is ever installed without \fB\-\-install\fR.
.TP
\fBupdate\-lists\fR [\fB\-\-list\fR] [\fB\-\-yes\fR]
Download fresh \fIpci.ids\fR (pci\-ids.ucw.cz), \fIusb.ids\fR (linux\-usb.org) and \fIpnp.ids\fR (hwdata) with curl (or wget) into \fI~/.local/share/srs/lists\fR (\fI$XDG_DATA_HOME/srs/lists\fR when set); no root. Each download is checked (header, enough entries) before it is used, a failed one leaves the copy in use, and the previous copy is kept as \fI.old\fR. Every lookup then uses the newer of these and the system's copies, by the list's own version. \fB\-\-list\fR only shows the copies in use and their versions; asks first (\fBGo on? [y/N]\fR) unless \fB\-\-yes\fR. Exit 0 when at least one list was updated or checked, 1 when nothing was downloaded, 2 on a bad option.
.TP
\fBgpu\-top\fR | \fBbig\-top\fR [\fB\-\-list\fR|\fB\-\-dump\fR|\fB\-\-gpu\-metrics\fR|\fB\-\-drm\-info\fR|\fB\-\-pp\-table\fR|\fB\-\-process\fR|\fB\-\-xdna\fR|\fB\-\-smi\fR|\fB\-\-tui\fR] [\fB\-J\fR|\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-device\fR \fIN\fR [\fB\-\-wake\fR]] [\fB\-\-pc\fR] [\fB\-\-interval\fR \fIMS\fR] [\fB\-n\fR \fICOUNT\fR]
The Big Top's GPU view (2.5; \fBbig\-top\fR is the same command): what each GPU is doing now, a C reimplementation of amdgpu_top (AMD, MIT licence, Umio Yasuno) with NVIDIA cards through NVML. Read-only: it never changes a clock, a voltage, a fan or a power limit. \fB\-\-list\fR the GPUs; \fB\-\-dump\fR (\fB\-d\fR) device info (chip, shaders, clocks, VRAM, caches, sensors, IP versions, firmware, video caps, VBIOS); \fB\-\-gpu\-metrics\fR every gpu_metrics field; \fB\-\-drm\-info\fR connectors, modes and properties; \fB\-\-pp\-table\fR the PowerPlay table (RX 5000/6000/7000); \fB\-\-process\fR processes with VRAM / GTT; \fB\-\-xdna\fR the XDNA NPU; \fB\-\-smi\fR one compact block per GPU after one interval; \fB\-\-tui\fR a live terminal view (the default on a terminal, else \fB\-\-smi\fR; q quits). \fB\-J\fR prints amdgpu_top-style JSON, alone one sample per interval (\fB\-n\fR \fICOUNT\fR, 0 = forever). \fB\-\-device\fR (\fB\-i\fR) one GPU; \fB\-\-pc\fR reads the GRBM counters for \fB\-\-smi\fR, \fB\-\-tui\fR and the \fB\-J\fR stream (off by default, as in the window; \fB\-\-no\-pc\fR is accepted and does nothing); \fB\-\-interval\fR (\fB\-s\fR) 100..60000 ms, default 1000, kept exactly (10 ms steps). A sleeping (runtime suspended) GPU is not opened: it is listed as "asleep (runtime suspended) \- not read"; \fB\-\-device\fR \fIN\fR alone on it exits 2, \fB\-\-device\fR \fIN\fR \fB\-\-wake\fR wakes and reads it. libdrm_amdgpu, libdrm and libnvidia\-ml are loaded at run time only when needed. An awake card with no user is left free to fall asleep. Exit 0 printed, 1 no GPU or it could not be opened, 2 usage error (also \fB\-\-device\fR \fIN\fR on a sleeping card without \fB\-\-wake\fR).
.TP
\fBir\fR [\fB\-\-status\fR|\fB\-\-list\fR|\fB\-\-send\fR \fIREMOTE BUTTON\fR|\fB\-\-send\-code\fR \fIHEX\fR [\fB\-\-nec\fR]|\fB\-\-learn\fR [\fB\-\-save\fR \fIREMOTE BUTTON\fR]|\fB\-\-probe\fR|\fB\-\-reset\fR|\fB\-\-export\fR \fIFILE\fR|\fB\-\-import\fR \fIFILE\fR [\fB\-\-replace\fR]] [\fB\-\-repeat\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-frequency\fR \fIHZ\fR] [\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fIS\fR] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-\-huffman\fR] [\fB\-\-yes\fR]
IR Blaster (2.5): an ELK Smart / OCRUSTAR USB infrared dongle (045c:0132, 0184, 0195, 0134) as a remote control. \fB\-\-status\fR (default, read-only) whether one is plugged in and usable (else how to install the udev rule 70\-elksmart\-ir.rules); \fB\-\-list\fR the saved remotes and codes (IR Remote's ~/.config/irremote/remotes.json, shared with that app; a one-time copy remotes.json.before\-srs is kept); \fB\-\-send\fR a saved code (\fB\-\-repeat\fR 1..100); \fB\-\-send\-code\fR a hex or NEC code; \fB\-\-learn\fR one button held within 1 cm (\fB\-\-timeout\fR 1..600 s, default 20; \fB\-\-save\fR keeps it); \fB\-\-probe\fR / \fB\-\-reset\fR send no infrared (\fB\-\-probe\fR exits 1 when the dongle is busy); \fB\-\-export\fR / \fB\-\-import\fR the codes. \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR only prints the USB packets. \fB\-\-send\fR and \fB\-\-learn\fR ask first unless \fB\-\-yes\fR. Exit 0 done, 1 no usable dongle / not confirmed / failed, 2 usage error.
.PP
\fBburn\fR, \fBbench\fR, \fBipi\fR, \fBlatency\fR, \fBpi\fR, \fBcrypto\fR, \fBprime\fR, \fBcachemem\fR, \fBstorage\fR, \fBos\-latency\fR, \fBcompute\fR, \fBwork\fR, \fBnet\fR, \fBgpu\fR, \fBgpu\-burn\fR and \fBgpu\-compare\fR load the CPU, the drive, the network or the GPU and only run when asked. All of them except \fBburn\fR (the burn\-in itself) also ask before they start, on stderr (\fBStart now? [y/N]\fR), unless \fB\-\-yes\fR is given; without a terminal and without \fB\-\-yes\fR they exit with 2 and start nothing. Use them at your own risk: SRS comes without any warranty, and its authors are not responsible for any damage (see the licence, GNU GPL v2); the question, \fB\-\-help\fR and \fBburn\fR print this notice, and \fB\-\-yes\fR counts as agreeing to it. \fB\-\-help\fR after a command prints its usage; an unknown option exits with 2 without starting. Only one load runs at a time: when another SRS process holds \fI$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/srs/bench.lock\fR (a live PID; a stale one is ignored), these commands ask first, refuse without a terminal (exit 2), and with \fB\-\-yes\fR warn and start.
Run
.B sysreport-search \-\-help
for the full option list.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SRS_DOC_DIR
directory holding SRS-User-Guide.pdf and html/ (default /usr/share/doc/srs-sysinfo).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/doc/srs-sysinfo/
the user guide (SRS-User-Guide.pdf, html/SRS-User-Guide.html), CLI-REFERENCE.md and GLOSSARY.md.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR srs-gui (1),
.BR srs-sysreport (1)
